<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perfeddwlad</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Berfeddwlad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Perfeddwlad, the &quot;Middle Country&quot;, was the name for the four cantrefi of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-conwy">River Conwy</a> and the River Dee: Rhos, Rhufoniog, Dyffryn Clwyd and Tegeingl. Lying between Gwynedd and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, it was repeatedly fought over by Welsh princes and English kings in the 12th and 13th centuries. After the conquest of 1283 it was divided between marcher lordships and the new county of Flint.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>For much of its history the land between the Conwy and the Dee was called Tegeingl, after the Deceangli, a Celtic people who had lived in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>; the name also belonged to its easternmost cantref. As the Kingdom of Gwynedd came to dominate North Wales the area was also known as Gwynedd Is Conwy, Gwynedd below the Conwy. The name Y Berfeddwlad, from perfedd and gwlad, heart-land or middle country, appears in the High Middle Ages, as rivalry between Gwynedd, Powys and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> sharpened over it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-gwynedd">Owain Gwynedd</a> his sons divided his lands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a> and the Perfeddwlad went to Dafydd. In 1194 Llywelyn Fawr, Owain's grandson, defeated Dafydd at the Battle of Aberconwy and took the Perfeddwlad. After Llywelyn's death King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a> invaded it to force his son Dafydd to accept the Treaty of Gwerneigron, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-woodstock">Treaty of Woodstock</a> of 1247 Henry took the Perfeddwlad and gave it to his son Edward.</p><p>About a decade later its people, aggrieved at Edward's rule, appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llywelyn-ap-gruffudd">Llywelyn ap Gruffudd</a>, who invaded and captured it in late 1256. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-montgomery">Treaty of Montgomery</a> of 1269 recognised his hold on it and his title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>. After Edward, now king, attacked in 1277, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-aberconwy">Treaty of Aberconwy</a> divided the Perfeddwlad: Edward kept the coastal cantrefi and Llywelyn's brother Dafydd received Rhufoniog and Dyffryn Clwyd.</p><p>In 1282 Dafydd attacked the new English castles of Hawarden and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhuddlan">Rhuddlan</a>, and Llywelyn joined him. The revolt was crushed, Llywelyn was killed in battle and Dafydd was executed. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-rhuddlan">Statute of Rhuddlan</a> of 1284, Rhos and Rhufoniog became the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbigh">Denbigh</a> under Henry de Lacy, Earl of Lincoln, Dyffryn Clwyd the lordship of Ruthin under Reginald de Grey, and Tegeingl the lordship of Englefield and the core of the county of Flint, under the palatinate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Perfeddwlad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rhos was later firmly part of the Kingdom of Gwynedd as a cantref of the <b>Perfeddwlad</b>, contested with English kings, until the conquest of 1283.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-rhos">Kingdom of Rhos</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 9th century Rhufoniog had been absorbed into the Kingdom of Gwynedd and later formed one of the cantrefi of the <b>Perfeddwlad</b>, the borderland between Gwynedd and England contested by Welsh princes and English kings until 1283.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-rhufoniog">Kingdom of Rhufoniog</a></li></ul></section>
